+# Design analysis for the D14 variant
+# -----------------------------------
+# a) Concrete behavior
+#    - The legend stays visually identical: a 6x24 top band with 4 colored bars,
+#      where left/right placement still means CCW/CW.
+#    - The main grid still has one solid teal square and 3-4 edge-touching obstacle
+#      rectangles whose colors are drawn from the legend.
+#    - The variant change is not a new marker but a new spatial condition:
+#      exactly one cardinal corridor from the teal square lines up with an obstacle.
+#      That unique aligned side is the only legal launch direction.
+#    - After the first hit, the ray keeps going as one continuous sz-wide polyline.
+#      Example: source launches north, hits the north obstacle, turns east, hits the
+#      east obstacle, turns south, hits the south obstacle, then exits. The original
+#      task would also paint west/south/east spokes from the source in parallel;
+#      this variant paints only that chained path.
+# b) Complexity calibration
+#    - To keep the change obvious, every generated case has a chain length of 3 or 4
+#      obstacle hits. That is longer than a trivial one-bounce simplification.
+#    - The start rule is supported by repeated visual evidence: only one obstacle
+#      initially covers the source lane, while the other present sides visibly do not.
+# c) Implicit assumptions from the old main()
+#    - The old `for d0 in range(4)` hard-coded four independent launches; this must
+#      be replaced by discovering a unique hittable initial direction.
+#    - The old logic tolerated multiple directly hittable sides because every side
+#      was launched anyway. In the variant that would be ambiguous, so the generator
+#      guarantees uniqueness and the solver asserts it.
+#    - The turning rule itself remains valid: obstacle colors still map to L/R in
+#      the legend, and collision still happens before entering the obstacle cell.
+# d) Placement legality
+#    - Obstacles remain non-overlapping rectangles touching grid edges.
+#    - Adjacent-side corner overlap is prevented by excluding corner intervals when
+#      choosing spans.
+#    - The teal square keeps at least one cell of separation from every obstacle.
+# e) Visual observability
+#    - No hidden ordering by color id is used. The only new control variable is the
+#      visibly unique corridor from the source to the first obstacle.
+#    - Outputs differ clearly from the original: they show a single bent trail rather
+#      than a four-armed cross.
+# f) Visual cleanliness
+#    - Chains are restricted to simple perimeter walks of length 3 or 4, which keeps
+#      the polyline readable and avoids clutter from self-intersections.
+# g) Color inference
+#    - Colors still only encode turn direction through the legend; the variant does
+#      not introduce any extra color semantics.
+# 9) New solver assumptions and generator support
+#    - `_find_start_direction()` assumes exactly one direction hits an obstacle before
+#      exiting. `_build_constraints()` enforces that by requiring source alignment for
+#      only the first side and excluding it for the others.
+#    - `_trace_chain()` assumes the path does not cycle. The generator uses a simple
+#      non-repeating side sequence and an exit turn on the last obstacle.
+#    - `_obstacle_color_at()` assumes each beam footprint sees at most one obstacle
+#      color at once. The generator keeps obstacle rectangles disjoint.
